WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.951
Binary protocols are not only collections of fields.

2
00:00:02.951 --> 00:00:04.795
Many protocols also have state.

3
00:00:04.795 --> 00:00:13.648
A message may be valid only during setup, only after authentication, only after a capability was negotiated, or only before a stream has closed.

4
00:00:13.648 --> 00:00:21.394
A parser that checks shape but ignores state can accept messages at times when the application contract does not allow them.

5
00:00:21.394 --> 00:00:24.345
State machines should be explicit enough to test.

6
00:00:24.345 --> 00:00:30.615
The implementation should define allowed transitions, required prior messages, terminal states, retry behavior, and cleanup on failure.

7
00:00:30.615 --> 00:00:39.099
When state changes happen in scattered code, reviewers have a harder time confirming that invalid sequences are rejected and that partial state is

8
00:00:39.099 --> 00:00:39.837
cleaned up.

9
00:00:39.837 --> 00:00:41.312
Versioning adds another layer.

10
00:00:41.312 --> 00:00:49.058
Binary protocols often evolve over years, so parsers should be clear about which versions, flags, optional fields, and feature combinations they

11
00:00:49.058 --> 00:00:49.427
understand.

12
00:00:49.427 --> 00:00:52.378
Unsupported versions should fail safely with useful diagnostics.

13
00:00:52.378 --> 00:01:00.124
Unknown fields may be ignored, rejected, or preserved depending on the contract, but that behavior should be deliberate rather than accidental.

14
00:01:00.124 --> 00:01:03.075
Reserved fields are a design choice, not filler.

15
00:01:03.075 --> 00:01:09.345
A protocol might require them to be zero, preserve them for forwarding, or reject them when set.

16
00:01:09.345 --> 00:01:15.985
Whatever the rule is, tests should lock it down so later compatibility work does not silently weaken validation.

17
00:01:15.985 --> 00:01:18.935
Compatibility should help evolution without accepting undefined behavior.

18
00:01:18.935 --> 00:01:27.419
A secure parser can support old and new message forms, but it should still enforce framing, lengths, numeric ranges, state rules, and authentication

19
00:01:27.419 --> 00:01:29.263
requirements for every supported version.

20
00:01:29.263 --> 00:01:37.009
Version tests should include both accepted and rejected cases, so maintainers can extend the protocol without guessing which historical behavior is

21
00:01:37.009 --> 00:01:40.329
still required in deployed systems and long-lived clients.

22
00:01:40.329 --> 00:01:43.280
Compatibility must remain explicit and testable over time.
